"Then we x it and keep going. General, you asked for a quarter billion dollars to
build something that could help humanity survive. I want to see survival, not
cautious incrementalism."
"What exactly are you proposing?"
"Accelerated integration. Facility systems in month one, regional systems in month
two, national infrastructure in month three. Aggressive timeline, but necessary
timeline."
"That's three times faster than our current plan."
"Then improve your current plan. General, I didn't fund this project so we could
spend years proving AGI is safe. I funded it so we could use AGI to solve problems.
Make it happen."
She hung up.
Marcus stared at the phone. Then at the integration schedule. Then at the clock
showing 3:47 PM, which meant he'd been awake for nineteen hours and had at leastsix more
hours of work before he could sleep.
He pulled up a blank document and started drafting the revised integration plan.
The technical challenges were immense. The political optics were terrible. The risk
of catastrophic failure was non-trivial.
But Morrison was right about one thing: they didn't have time for perfection.
They needed capability, reliability, and institutional trust. And they needed all three
simultaneously.
---
The facility integration meeting happened at 7 AM the next morning. Marcus had
invited every key stakeholder: Elizabeth and her technical team, Colonel Mendez
representing operations, Dr. Sarah Chen from the original MIT program,
representatives from the six federal agencies requesting integration.
He'd sent the revised timeline ahead of time. Now he got to watch people's
reactions.
"This is insane," Chen said atly. "You're proposing to connect an unprecedented AI
system to critical national infrastructure in three months. That's not aggressive.
That's reckless."
"It's necessary," Marcus countered. "Senator Morrison is right that we don't have the
luxury of slow deployment. Climate models show—"

"I've seen the climate models, General. I've also seen the failure modes for rushed AI
deployment. We're talking about giving Prometheus control over systems wheremistakes could kill
people."
"Not control. Guidance. With human oversight and emergency cutoffs."
"Semantics. If Prometheus is optimizing power distribution and makes an error,
people die. If he's coordinating disaster response and miscalculates, people die. The
