Elizabeth spoke up. "Dr. Chen is right about the risks. But she's also right that the
alternative is worse. If we don't accelerate deployment, if we spend years on gradual
integration, the problems we're trying to solve will become unsolvable."
"So we're choosing between denite slow catastrophe and possible fast
catastrophe?"
"We're choosing to try," Marcus said. "Which means accepting risk in pursuit of
necessary capability."
He pulled up the revised integration plan. "Here's how we manage the risk. Month
one: facility systems only. Prometheus manages internal power, water, HVAC,
security. Fully contained environment where failures affect only us."
"That's already in the plan," Jeremiah noted.
"Yes. But we accelerate the evaluation timeline. Instead of two months proving
reliability, we take two weeks. If performance is solid, we move to phase two
immediately."
"Two weeks isn't enough time to evaluate emergent problems," Chen objected."It's enough to
evaluate basic functionality. Emergent problems will appear
regardless of how long we test. Better to encounter them in controlled deployment
