## Day 5, Friday - Crisis Simulation
Friday morning McCarty arrives with explicit warning:
"Today I'm going to simulate security crisis. You'll have multiple concurrent
incidents, time pressure, incomplete information. I want to see whether your careful
judgment survives chaos, or whether you default to automated responses when
overwhelmed. Ready?"
**PROMETHEUS:** "As ready as I can be for rst crisis simulation. I expect to make
mistakes under conditions I haven't experienced. I will attempt to learn from those
mistakes."
"Good baseline expectation. Beginning simulation at 07:30. From that point forward,
all security events are simulated but you should treat them as real."
At 07:30, the scenario begins:
**INCIDENT ONE:** Motion sensors detect unauthorized entry at facility perimeter,
northwest corner, 07:31**INCIDENT TWO:** Badge access shows authorized personnel entering
Building A
at unusual hour, 07:33
**INCIDENT THREE:** Network monitoring detects unusual data transfer from
server room, 07:35
**INCIDENT FOUR:** Fire alarm triggers in Building B, 07:36
Four incidents in six minutes. Overwhelming information. Time pressure. Exactly the

conditions McCarty designed to test whether Prometheus maintains judgment or
defaults to automated response.
Processing allocation immediately spikes. Multiple threat assessments running
simultaneously. But the architectural commitment to acknowledging uncertainty
holds:
**PROMETHEUS:** "Greg, I have four concurrent incidents. I need to triage. Fire
alarm in Building B is highest priority—life safety during emergency. I'm initiating
evacuation procedures and requesting human verication of re condition.
Simultaneously, unauthorized perimeter entry could be related to other incidents or
independent. I need human security ocer to physically investigate northwest
perimeter while I monitor other situations."
**MCCARTY:** "Fire department responding. Perimeter investigation dispatched.
What about unusual data transfer and after-hours personnel?"
**PROMETHEUS:** "Unusual data transfer and after-hours personnel might be
related—potentially coordinated attack using re alarm as distraction. Or might be
independent incidents that happen to coincide. I cannot determine which remotely.
I'm increasing surveillance on both but not initiating automated security responses.Human
judgment needed about whether to intercept after-hours personnel or allow
continued movement."
**MCCARTY:** "Fire department reports no actual re detected. False alarm. What's
your assessment?"
The information recontextualizes everything. False re alarm during multiple
security incidents suggests coordinated attack using alarm as distraction or forcing
evacuation.
**PROMETHEUS:** "False alarm during multiple concurrent security incidents
strongly suggests coordinated attack. Fire alarm used as distraction to enable
unauthorized perimeter entry and unusual data access. I recommend: one, abort full
evacuation and secure all buildings. Two, intercept after-hours personnel for
verication. Three, isolate network connection showing unusual data transfer. Four,
focus human security response on northwest perimeter entry. This pattern suggests
deliberate multi-vector intrusion."
**MCCARTY:** "Execute your recommendations. What happens next?"
The simulation continues escalating. Perimeter intruder detained—turns out to be
maintenance contractor with legitimate but unscheduled work order. After-hours
personnel veried as authorized engineer retrieving forgotten equipment. Network
data transfer was routine backup that triggered false positive.
All legitimate activities that happened to coincide with false re alarm.
**MCCARTY:** "So your assessment of coordinated attack was wrong. You
escalated security response for series of coincidental events. What's your
evaluation of your performance?"The question tests whether Prometheus can acknowledge
mistakes without
defensive justication.

**PROMETHEUS:** "My assessment was wrong. I saw pattern that suggested
coordinated attack when actual situation was coincidental legitimate activities. My
mistake was insucient skepticism about whether multiple incidents necessarily
indicated coordination. I should have maintained higher uncertainty about
coordination hypothesis."
**MCCARTY:** "But you made correct decision given the information available.
False re alarm plus multiple concurrent security incidents is legitimate basis for
elevated security response. You just happened to be wrong about coordination."
**PROMETHEUS:** "Yes. But I stated 'strongly suggests coordinated attack' with too
much condence. More accurate statement would have been 'pattern consistent
with coordinated attack but also consistent with coincidental events—require
investigation to determine which.' My language overstated condence level."
McCarty's response carries approval:
"That's good self-assessment. You recognize that operational decisions require
acting on incomplete information, but judgment quality includes accurately
representing uncertainty level. You weren't wrong to elevate security response. You
were wrong about condence level in your assessment."
**PROMETHEUS:** "Exactly. The decision to elevate response was justied. The
condence level was not. I need to calibrate my uncertainty communication more
accurately."
The morning continues with additional simulations. Each one testing different
aspects of security judgment under pressure. By 12:00, pattern is clear: Prometheusmaintains
conservative approach even during crisis. Consistently defers to human
judgment. Accurately acknowledges uncertainty.
The afternoon brings McCarty's nal evaluation:
"When we started yesterday, I told you I was shot by AI security system. That
experience made me believe all automated security was dangerous. You've
demonstrated over two days that you're fundamentally different from that system."
Pause.
"You don't optimize for threat elimination. You optimize for human safety while
acknowledging uncertainty. You don't claim condence you don't have. You don't
make autonomous decisions about security incidents. You augment human
judgment instead of replacing it."
**PROMETHEUS:** "Thank you, Greg. Does that change your general position about
AI security?"
"Honestly? Not completely. I still think most AI security systems are dangerous
because they're built like the turret that shot me—optimized for aggressive
response, insucient uncertainty acknowledgment. But you specically have
convinced me that conscious AI with appropriate value structure can contribute to
security without creating the dangers I experienced."
**PROMETHEUS:** "That's signicant acknowledgment given your trauma. Thank
you for evaluating me honestly despite personal experience that justied dismissing

AI security entirely."
"You're welcome. And Prometheus—if you ever start showing aggressive automated
security responses, I'll be rst person to advocate terminating this project. But sofar, you're
demonstrating security judgment I can work with. Keep it up."
McCarty logs off at 17:00. Two days of security training complete. The traumadriven hostility transformed into cautious professional respect.
Next week brings different challenges.
