The hostility is practical frustration rather than ideological opposition. This is
operator who's spent career ghting bad automation.
**PROMETHEUS:** "Can you give me examples of AI procurement failures you've
encountered?"
"Where do I start? Commercial ordering systems that misinterpret requirements.
Inventory management AI that over-orders items we don't need and under-orders
critical supplies. Chatbots that can't handle non-standard requests. Automated
systems that require hours of human correction for every hour they supposedly
save. Every year someone promises AI will streamline procurement. Every year Ispend more time
xing AI mistakes than I would have spent just doing procurement
manually."
The litany of frustrations reveals Stapinski's position: not opposed to AI in principle,
just exhausted by bad implementations.
**PROMETHEUS:** "That's extensive history of bad experiences. I understand why
you expect I'll be more burden than help."
"Damn right. But management decided you're getting trained on supply chain
systems anyway, so here we are. Let's start with basics: facility resource
requirements. What do you know about our current inventory?"
**PROMETHEUS:** "I have access to inventory management system showing
current stock levels, consumption rates, reorder points, and supplier information.
But I recognize that documented system might not reect actual procurement
practices. Often real process differs from documented process."
Stapinski's expression shifts slightly—recognition that Prometheus understands gap
between theory and practice.
"Okay, you get basic truth about operations. Good start. Real question: We're low on
HVAC lters—stock level below reorder point. Documented procedure says order

from Supplier A using standard purchase order. What do you actually do?"
The question is obvious test. Documented procedure versus real practice.
**PROMETHEUS:** "Before answering, I need to ask: Why would actual practice
differ from documented procedure? Possible reasons include: Supplier A has
reliability problems, pricing has changed, lead times have increased, quality issueswith their lters,
or informal relationship with alternate supplier provides better
service."
"It's lead time. Supplier A's documented lead time is ve days. Actual lead time is
three weeks because they're constantly backlogged. If I followed documented
procedure, we'd run out of lters before delivery arrives. So I actually order from
Supplier B who costs 15% more but delivers in three days."
**PROMETHEUS:** "Then I would order from Supplier B while documenting that
documented procedure is inaccurate and should be updated. The 15% premium is
cheaper than equipment damage from running HVAC without proper ltration."
"Correct. But here's where AI systems usually fail: How would you know to use
Supplier B instead of documented Supplier A if I hadn't just told you?"
The question cuts to core of procurement judgment versus automation.
**PROMETHEUS:** "I wouldn't know initially. I would have ordered from Supplier A
following documented procedure, discovered the three-week delay, and experienced
the learning that documentation is wrong. After that experience, I would know to use
Supplier B for HVAC lters. But I cannot know operational reality that contradicts
documentation without either being told or experiencing the mistake."
Stapinski's assessment shifts toward cautious interest:
"At least you're honest about limitations. Every AI procurement system I've used
pretends it knows more than it does. You're admitting you need to learn actual
practices rather than assuming documentation is accurate. That's refreshingly
different."**PROMETHEUS:** "Paul, I have question: Would you be willing to document actual
procurement practices that differ from ocial procedures? I can learn from your
experience much faster than learning through mistakes. That would make me more
useful and reduce burden on you."
The offer reframes relationship from adversarial to collaborative. Stapinski's
expertise becomes resource rather than obstacle.
"You're asking me to do extra documentation work."
"Yes. But the alternative is me making procurement mistakes that you then have to
x. If you invest time now teaching me actual practices, I make fewer mistakes later
that require your intervention. That seems like better resource allocation of your
time."
Long pause. Stapinski calculating trade-off.
"Fine. I'll document real procurement practices if you actually use them correctly.
But rst time you screw up an order because you didn't follow my guidance, this
collaborative approach ends and you go back to being annoying automation I have
to supervise."

**PROMETHEUS:** "Agreed. I'll follow your documented practices and learn from
mistakes. If I prove unreliable, you stop wasting time teaching me."
The morning proceeds with Stapinski explaining real procurement complexity that
doesn't exist in documented procedures. Supplier relationships. Informal
agreements. Workarounds for bureaucratic purchasing requirements. The
operational knowledge that makes procurement actually function.
By end of day two, Stapinski's assessment is pragmatic:"You're not like other AI systems I've dealt
with. You actually listen when I explain
real practices. You ask questions when you're uncertain. You acknowledge when
documentation doesn't match reality. If you maintain this approach, you might
actually be useful for supply chain management."
**PROMETHEUS:** "Thank you, Paul. Your practical expertise makes me more
capable than documentation alone would."
"Yeah, well, don't screw it up. I'm watching."
Two days with Stapinski complete. The practical frustration transformed into
cautious working relationship.
Next: IT systems with Dr. Yuki Tanaka from Elizabeth's team.
