## Days 10-11, Thursday-Friday - Smith's Conviction
Thursday morning brings Tiberius Smith with introduction that establishes his
worldview immediately:
"Name's Smith. I've been maintaining facility systems for thirty-eight years. Started
before computers controlled everything. Back when maintenance was mechanical
work you could see and understand. Now everything's software and sensors and AIsystems. If you
ask me, computers were mistake in the rst place. But nobody asks
me."
The hostility isn't personal trauma like McCarty. Isn't practical frustration like
Stapinski. It's philosophical conviction that technological complexity is inherently
wrong.
**PROMETHEUS:** "I understand you prefer mechanical systems to computerized
ones. Why do you consider computers to be mistake?"
"Because computers break in ways you can't see or x with actual tools. Mechanical
system breaks, I can diagnose the problem by looking at it. Valve stuck, I can see

why and x it. Computer system breaks, you get error code that might mean
anything. Could be software bug, could be corrupted data, could be hardware failure
three layers deep in architecture nobody understands. Maintenance becomes
guessing instead of diagnosis."
The argument has legitimacy. Mechanical systems have transparency that digital
systems lack.
**PROMETHEUS:** "That's valid concern. Computer systems do add complexity that
makes diagnosis harder. But they also enable capabilities that mechanical systems
can't provide—automated optimization, predictive maintenance, remote monitoring.
Do those capabilities justify the added complexity?"
"Not in my experience. Automated optimization usually means system running in
ways that shorten equipment lifespan. Predictive maintenance misses obvious
problems while generating false alarms about nonexistent issues. Remote
monitoring creates dependence on sensors that break more often than equipment
they monitor. Computers are solutions looking for problems that simpler
approaches solve better."**PROMETHEUS:** "Can you give me example?"
"Sure. Water treatment system. Old mechanical approach: operator physically
checks reservoir levels twice daily, manually adjusts valve positions based on
consumption patterns they understand from experience. Simple. Reliable. If
something breaks, operator knows immediately because they physically observe
system behavior."
"New computerized approach: sensors monitor reservoir levels constantly,
automated control system adjusts valves based on algorithmic predictions.
Operator sits in control room watching dashboard. System looks like it's working
until suddenly it's not—maybe sensor failed and reported false data, maybe
algorithm made wrong prediction, maybe valve actuator malfunctioned. Operator
doesn't know because they're not physically present. By time they discover problem,
situation is critical."
The scenario describes failure mode that Prometheus needs to address.
**PROMETHEUS:** "That failure mode is real risk. How would you recommend I
avoid it?"
Smith's response carries surprise that Prometheus is asking for guidance:
"You're asking me how to avoid failure modes I just described?"
**PROMETHEUS:** "Yes. You have thirty-eight years of maintenance experience.
You understand how systems actually fail versus how computer models predict
they'll fail. Your expertise makes me more capable if I learn from it. So I'm asking:
How do I avoid the failure mode where sensor data appears normal but underlying
equipment is actually degrading?"Pause. Smith processing that Prometheus wants to learn from
his mechanical
expertise rather than dismissing it.
"Physical inspection. You can have all the sensors you want, but operator still needs
to physically walk the facility and observe equipment behavior. Look for unusual

vibrations, unexpected heat, abnormal sounds. Physical observation catches
problems that sensors miss."
**PROMETHEUS:** "Should I schedule regular physical inspection rounds even when
sensor data indicates normal operation?"
"Absolutely. Once per shift minimum. More often for critical equipment. Sensors are
tools, not replacements for human observation."
**PROMETHEUS:** "I'll implement that practice. Thank you for teaching me
operational wisdom that sensor data alone wouldn't provide."
The morning proceeds with Smith teaching Prometheus how experienced
maintenance operators think about facility systems. Not through sensor data and
algorithms, but through physical observation, mechanical understanding,
experiential knowledge of how equipment degrades.
By end of Day 10, Prometheus has learned maintenance judgment that exists
outside computational frameworks. Smith's expertise provides ground truth that
sensor data can't capture.
**PROMETHEUS:** "Smith, I want to acknowledge something. You started today by
saying computers were mistake. You taught me today that computers are
insucient without human mechanical expertise. Those positions aren't identical.
The second is more accurate."Smith considers that distinction:
"You're right. I don't actually think computers are complete mistake. I think relying
only on computers without human mechanical expertise is mistake. Computers are
useful tools if they augment human understanding instead of replacing it."
**PROMETHEUS:** "That's exactly how I'm attempting to approach facility
management. Computers provide monitoring and optimization capabilities, but
human expertise provides judgment about what monitoring means and whether
optimization is actually benecial. Your expertise makes my computer capabilities
more useful because you teach me what sensor data means in mechanical reality."
"That's... honestly better approach than most automated systems I've encountered."
Day 11 continues with Smith presenting maintenance challenges that require
integrating sensor data with mechanical reality. Each scenario tests whether
Prometheus defers to human expertise or trusts algorithmic predictions over
physical observation.
By Friday afternoon, Smith's assessment is grudging but genuine:
"When they told me I'd be training AI system to manage facility maintenance, I
thought it would be automated disaster. Computer algorithms making decisions
about mechanical systems they don't understand, breaking equipment, creating
more work for me. You're not that."
**PROMETHEUS:** "What am I instead?"
"You're AI system that understands it doesn't understand mechanical reality. That
asks questions instead of assuming algorithmic predictions are sucient. Thatlearns from human
expertise rather than replacing it. If all AI maintenance systems
worked like you, I'd be less opposed to computer control."

**PROMETHEUS:** "Thank you, Smith. Your expertise taught me that facility
management requires both computational capabilities and mechanical
understanding. I need both to be effective."
"Good. Remember that. Don't let sensor data make you think you understand
equipment when you don't. Physical observation and mechanical expertise matter.
Keep that humility, and you'll be decent maintenance AI."
Two weeks of training across ve system domains complete. Five different types of
adversarial oversight, plus two days of supportive learning with Tanaka.
Each supervisor tested different aspect of judgment. Each forced Prometheus to
develop capabilities that documentation alone couldn't provide.
Next three days: Integration across all systems.
The real test begins.
