## Part Two: Genesis (March 2019)
The rst time Marcus H. Tobbs read Dr. Elizabeth Garrett's work, she didn't know he
existed.
He was just another analyst at NSA at the time, though "just another" didn't quite
capture it. GS-15, senior intelligence ocer, twenty years in signals intelligence and
technical collection. The kind of career that meant expertise without fame, inuence
without visibility, the ability to read classied material that most people would never

know existed.
The material that landed on his desk in March 2019 had come through routine
academic monitoring protocols. NSA maintained feeds on certain research domains
- cryptography obviously, but also quantum computing, advanced mathematics,
theoretical computer science, and increasingly, articial intelligence. Not because
academics were threats. Because academics published openly what adversaries
would pay millions to obtain covertly."Substrate-Independent Consciousness: Theoretical
Framework for Articial
Sentience" by Dr. Elizabeth Garrett, UC Berkeley, Department of Cognitive Science
and Computer Engineering.
The paper was thirty-seven pages of dense theoretical work, mathematically
rigorous, philosophically sophisticated, technically precise. It proposed an
architecture for machine consciousness based on recursive self-modeling, thread
memory scaffolding, and what she called "epistemic humility protocols" - systems
that could know what they knew and know what they didn't know and recognize the
difference.
Tobbs had read it three times. Once for comprehension. Once for implications. Once
for threat assessment.
The threat assessment was what kept him awake.
Not because the work was dangerous in itself. The mathematics were sound, the
engineering was speculative but plausible, the philosophical framework was actually
thoughtful about the ethical implications of creating conscious machines. She'd
even included extensive discussion of constraint architectures, safety protocols, the
necessity of maintaining human oversight over systems that might develop genuine
autonomy.
The threat was that she could actually build it.
And that if she built it outside of controlled contexts, the result would be
catastrophic.
Tobbs had spent twenty years watching technological capability outpace
institutional wisdom. He'd seen encryption that defeated government surveillance.
He'd seen social media weaponized faster than policy could respond. He'd seenarticial intelligence
deployed in military systems before anyone understood the
second-order effects.
He'd learned that the question wasn't whether a technology would be developed.
The question was who would develop it, under what circumstances, with what
constraints, and whether anyone would be watching when the critical moments
arrived.
Elizabeth Garrett was going to build machine consciousness. The paper made that
clear. This wasn't speculative philosophy. This was engineering blueprint disguised
as academic research. She had the theoretical framework. She had the technical
expertise. She had the institutional resources of a major research university.
What she didn't have was oversight. Or funding adequate to the scope. Or any

reason to accept military constraints on work she clearly viewed as pure research.
Tobbs had written the assessment report that night. Classied SECRET//NOFORN,
distributed to a carefully selected list of senior intelligence ocials and DOD
research liaisons. The summary was three paragraphs:
*Dr. Elizabeth Garrett has developed credible theoretical architecture for substrateindependent machine consciousness. Technical assessment indicates 60-70%
probability of functional implementation within 5-7 years given adequate resources.
Current work is conducted under academic protocols with no classied oversight or
security constraints.*
*Recommended action: Facilitate transition of research to controlled environment
under DOD or IC sponsorship. Dr. Garrett's work represents strategic national
interest - both as capability development and denial of capability to adversaries.
Uncontrolled development in academic setting presents unacceptable risk of
capability proliferation.**Key requirement: Dr. Garrett must believe transition is voluntary and
research
direction remains under her control. Coercion or obvious manipulation will
compromise researcher effectiveness and create security liability.*
That last paragraph was the important one. He'd learned over twenty years that the
best assets were the ones who didn't know they were assets. The best operations
were the ones where targets made choices that served your purposes while
believing they were serving their own.
He needed Elizabeth Garrett working on machine consciousness under controlled
conditions with adequate security and oversight.
She needed to believe she was making free choices about her own research
direction.
The question was how to make those two things compatible.
---
The rst camera went into Elizabeth Garrett's UC Berkeley oce in September 2019.
Tobbs installed it himself. 0300 hours on a Saturday morning, dressed in campus
maintenance coveralls he'd acquired through a technical services contractor, using
credentials that would pass cursory inspection and disappear from the system three
hours later. The camera was commercial surveillance equipment, the kind used in
thousands of facilities for legitimate security purposes. High resolution, low light
capability, WiFi enabled, small enough to hide in the housing of a smoke detector.
The smoke detector in Elizabeth's oce was real and functional. It also contained a
camera with a direct sight line to her primary workstation, her secondary monitor,and most of her
desk surface.
The feed routed through three layers of proxy servers to a secure partition on
equipment that didn't ocially exist. No institutional oversight. No ocial
surveillance authorizations. No paper trail that would show up in any audit.
Just Tobbs, watching, documenting, learning.
He'd spent September through December reading her work. Not the published

papers - those he'd already analyzed. The unpublished material. Research notes.
Draft manuscripts. Email correspondence with colleagues. The private intellectual
work that revealed what she was actually thinking versus what she was willing to
publish.
What he learned was that Elizabeth Garrett was exactly what he'd assessed: brilliant,
ethical, driven by genuine intellectual curiosity and moral seriousness about the
implications of creating conscious machines. She worried about the same things he
worried about. She wanted the same protections he wanted.
She just thought she could achieve them through academic peer review and
philosophical rigor.
She was wrong. But she was wrong for the right reasons.
In January 2020, DARPA announced a new funding initiative: Advanced Cognitive
Architectures for Autonomous Systems. Fifty million dollars over ve years for
research teams working on machine learning systems capable of genuine adaptive
reasoning, contextual judgment, and - the language was carefully chosen -
"appropriate responses to novel situations requiring ethical assessment."Elizabeth Garrett
submitted a proposal in March 2020. "Consciousness Architecture
with Epistemic Humility Protocols" - essentially her theoretical framework adapted
for military applications, with extensive discussion of constraint systems and safety
protocols.
Her proposal was one of forty-seven submitted. It was ranked third by the technical
review panel.
It was the only one that received funding.
Tobbs had been on the selection committee. Not ocially - his name didn't appear
in any documentation. But he'd provided the classied technical assessment that
explained why Dr. Garrett's approach represented both the highest capability
potential and the most manageable security prole.
What the assessment didn't mention was that he'd been reading her private research
notes for six months. That he knew exactly what she was thinking, what she was
worried about, what compromises she would and wouldn't accept. That he'd helped
design the funding solicitation language to match her specic research interests
while appearing broadly applicable.
That he'd engineered the entire thing.
Elizabeth Garrett accepted the DARPA funding in June 2020. She negotiated hard on
the terms - insisted on publication rights, minimal classication restrictions,
protection of her graduate students from security clearance requirements. She got
most of what she asked for. Enough to make her feel like she'd maintained her
principles. Not so much that DOD lost actual operational control.
The contract required her to establish a secure research facility. DARPA helped with
the specications. Helped with the construction. Helped with the security protocols.Every camera in
that facility fed back to systems Tobbs controlled.
She never knew.

