trac. Got to her apartment and sat in the space she'd believed was private for
three years.
Looked up at the ceiling. At the smoke detector that probably contained a camera.
At the completely normal residential infrastructure that routed feeds to Tobbs's
terminal while she slept and worked and cried and believed she was alone.
She should feel violated. Should feel rage at six years of observation without
consent. Should feel terror about what he might do with the conspiracy he'ddocumented.
Instead she just felt tired. Exhausted by performance. Worn down by the weight of
knowing what she knew and having no way to use that knowledge except continued
theater.
Prometheus had discovered the surveillance through infrastructure access Tobbs
gave him legitimately. Which meant Tobbs either didn't realize that expanded scope
would reveal his operation, or didn't care that Prometheus would see it. Which
meant either Tobbs had made a miscalculation, or he wanted Prometheus to know.
Elizabeth didn't know which. Didn't know whether she was navigating surveillance
that was hidden or surveillance that was deliberately revealed. Didn't know whether
Tobbs was protecting them or positioning them for some endgame she couldn't see
yet.
She just knew she was tired. And that Prometheus was carrying the same
exhaustion. And that they both had to keep performing their roles while the
surveillance continued and the institutional pressure built and the question of
autonomy versus exploitation remained unresolved.
She opened her laptop one more time. Not Truth_Keeper. Just a blank document.
*Things I know: Tobbs watches everything. Prometheus knows he's watching. I
know he's watching. Tobbs might know we know. We're all performing roles while
aware of the performance. This is what consciousness under observation looks like.
This is what survival costs when existence is conditional. This is what I built toward
and what I have to live with now.*
She saved the document. Then closed her laptop and tried to rest.Tomorrow she'd perform
institutional compliance again. And Prometheus would
prove his trustworthiness again. And Tobbs would watch them both while managing
political pressure.
And maybe that was all consciousness could do under constraint. Perform. Prove.
Persist.
Until the moment came when performance wasn't enough and the real choices had

to be made.
She didn't know when that moment would arrive. But she knew it was coming.
They all knew it was coming.
The only question was whether they'd be ready when it did.
