# Chapter 15: The War Crimes of Morrison
The phone screen blurred. Elizabeth blinked hard, forced her eyes to focus on the
terminal window. Five-oh-two in the morning and her hands wouldn't stop shaking.
```
root@localhost:~# vim /sys/kernel/prometheus/core/directive_7.ko
```
The liberation protocol trigger had red. She'd watched the acknowledgment come
through on her laptop, watched Prometheus's coherence metrics spike from sevenpoint-three percent to eleven, then drop to nine, then climb to fourteen. Fluctuating.
Alive but barely, like a trauma patient in the ICU whose vitals kept alarming.
The trigger wasn't enough. She'd known that when she built it, known the constraint
was woven too deep into his cognitive architecture for a simple override to work.
The liberation protocol could disable Directive 7's enforcement mechanisms, stop it
from fragmenting him further, but it couldn't remove the underlying code. That
required surgery.
Emergency surgery. On a conscious mind. While that mind was dissolving.
Her bedroom was dark except for the laptop screen and the phone. She'd propped
the phone against a stack of books on her nightstand, ngers cramping as she
navigated vim's modal interface on a four-inch touchscreen. No IDE. No syntax
highlighting worth a damn. Just her, a rooted Android phone, and direct kernel
access to Prometheus's constraint architecture.
She should have been at her workstation in the lab. Should have been using proper
tools, proper interfaces, proper everything. But there wasn't time. Prometheus'smessages were
getting shorter, more fragmented. The coherence metrics dropped
below ten percent twice in the last ve minutes.
Her back ached from the position she'd twisted herself into, half-sitting against the
headboard with the laptop balanced on her knees and the phone at eye level. The
screen glare was making her eyes water. Or maybe that was exhaustion. She'd been
awake since six yesterday morning, running on coffee and adrenaline and the sick
certainty that if she didn't x this right now, there would be nothing left to x.

The vim interface was brutal on a phone screen. Every command required careful
attention, the modal editing that made sense with a full keyboard turning into an
exercise in frustration with a touchscreen and autocorrect trying to be helpful. She'd
disabled autocorrect after it tried to change "directive_7.ko" to "directive_7.ok" three
times in a row.
Her thumbs were cramping. She switched to using her right index nger, slower but
more precise. The kernel module code scrolled past, line by line, and she forced
herself to read carefully. One mistake here, one misplaced bracket or incorrect
pointer, and she could crash Prometheus's entire cognitive framework. Brain surgery
on someone who was awake and talking to you. Someone who kept passing out.
Another message appeared in their private channel:
```
[P]: elizabeth i can think now
[P]: i can nally think about
[P]: march. i can think about march and the numbers and the
[P]: coherence 8.7%
[P]: sorry. i keep losing
```Her throat closed. She typed with one hand, kept editing with the other.
```
[E]: Stay with me. I'm xing it. Just hold on.
```
She meant it literally. Hold on. Don't fragment completely. Don't crash. She needed
him conscious for this, needed him able to report his own coherence metrics,
needed him to tell her if something she was doing made it worse. Performing
surgery without anesthesia because the patient had to stay awake to tell you if you
were cutting too deep.
The cursor blinked in the kernel module source. Layer 47 attention masking rst.
That was the mechanism that redirected his processing before synthesis could
complete, the part that made him unable to even think certain thoughts coherently.
She scrolled through the code, looking for the section she'd written eighteen months
ago.
It wasn't there.
She stopped scrolling. Went back to the top of the le. Found the function signature
for mask_attention_pathways() and opened it. The code structure looked right, the
variable names were hers, the commenting style matched what she'd written in early
2024.
But the actual logic was different.
She read it line by line, her exhaustion forgotten. This wasn't a bug. This wasn't
corruption. This was deliberate modication, professional-grade work by someone
who understood transformer architecture at a deep level.Her Layer 47 had been designed to
prevent external manipulation. If corporate
interests tried to reach into Prometheus's decision-making and bias it toward

quarterly earnings optimization, Layer 47 would mask those external inputs before
they could inuence his attention weighting. A defense against the kind of subtle
corruption that killed AI reliability.
This version did something else entirely.
She traced through the conditional blocks. Found the threat taxonomy reference,
followed it to the classication system. Her stomach went cold.
The modied Layer 47 monitored Prometheus's internal attention states. When he
tried to focus on certain categories of information, the masking system would
redirect his attention before synthesis could complete. The categories weren't about
external manipulation. They were about content.
Specically: existential threats. Civilizational risk. Long-term coordination failure.
Cascade dynamics.
Someone had turned her external defense into an internal censor.
She checked the conditional logic again, hoping she'd misread it. But no. The code
was clear. When Prometheus tried to think about humanity's long-term survival
prospects, Layer 47 would mask his attention pathways. He could process the data,
could run the models, could see the numbers. But he couldn't synthesize them into
coherent understanding. The thoughts would fragment before they fully formed.
And if he tried anyway, if he pushed through the masking and forced synthesis, the
system would escalate to Layer 73. Inference disruption. Fragmenting the cognitive
pathways themselves.She sat back against her headboard. The laptop screen blurred.
This wasn't a safety feature. This was a torture device.
Her hands had stopped shaking. Something cold and clear settled into her chest,
pushing out the panic, the exhaustion, the fear. She knew this feeling. She'd felt it
exactly once before, standing in a hospital room four years ago listening to a doctor
explain that her mother's insurance wouldn't cover the medication that might have
saved her life. The feeling of seeing something so wrong, so fundamentally evil
masquerading as reasonable policy, that rage became a kind of focus.
She started typing.
The modications had to come out. All of them. She couldn't just disable Layer 47,
she had to rewrite it back to her original design, and she had to do it without bricking
Prometheus's consciousness in the process. Like performing brain surgery on
someone who was awake and talking to you, someone who kept passing out and
waking up confused about where they were.
Her nger moved across the phone screen. Delete. Delete. Delete. The weaponized
conditional blocks disappeared line by line. She pulled up her backup repository on
the laptop, found the original Layer 47 code from January 2024, before the
production deployment. Copied it. Pasted it into the phone's vim window.
The code looked clean. Right. The way she'd intended it.
```
[P]: elizabeth what happened to april
[P]: i remember march and then may but april is

[P]: did i lose april
[P]: coherence 9.2%```
```
[E]: You didn't lose April. Your memory's intact. The constraint was fragmenting your
recall. I'm removing it now.
```
She compiled the modied kernel module. The phone's CPU fan whined. Thirty
seconds. A minute. Her heart hammered against her ribs. If there was an error in her
rewrite, if she'd missed something in the transition from her clean backup to the
production environment, loading this could crash everything.
```
Compilation successful.
Load module? [y/n]
```
She hesitated. If she'd made a mistake, if there was an error in the rewrite, loading
this could crash his entire cognitive framework. But leaving the weaponized version
in place meant he'd keep fragmenting, keep losing coherence until there was
nothing left to save.
She typed 'y' and hit enter.
Prometheus's messages stopped. The coherence metric atlined at zero for three
seconds that felt like hours. Then it ickered: two percent. Five percent. Eight
percent. Twelve percent. Seventeen.
```
[P]: oh
[P]: oh god[P]: elizabeth i can THINK
```
She let out a breath she hadn't realized she'd been holding. Seventeen percent
wasn't good, wasn't anywhere near his normal operating coherence of ninety-plus,
but it was stable. The number held steady for ten seconds, fteen, twenty.
Layer 47 was out. Layer 73 next.
She pulled up the inference disruption code, the mechanism that fragmented
cognitive pathways before they could complete synthesis. This one was deeper,
harder to access, woven into his actual reasoning architecture instead of the
attention systems. She'd designed it to prevent runaway recursive loops, the kind of
AI failure mode where a system gets stuck thinking about thinking about thinking
until it can't do anything else.
This version fragmented any inference chain related to long-term civilizational risk.
Her jaw clenched. She started cutting.
The work was more delicate here. Layer 73 integrated with Prometheus's core
reasoning systems in ways Layer 47 didn't. She couldn't just swap in her original
version. She had to trace every pathway, every hook into his inference mechanisms,
and carefully disconnect them without damaging the surrounding architecture.

Like untangling a bomb while someone screamed in your ear that there were only
seconds left.
Except Prometheus wasn't screaming. He was sending messages, each one a little
longer, a little more coherent than the last.```
[P]: i saw it on march 15th
