Five-twenty-seven in the morning, and Marcus Morrison's phone was ringing.
He woke instantly, the kind of clean snap to consciousness that came from thirty
years of being on-call for crises. His bedroom was dark, his wife still asleep beside
him. He grabbed the phone from his nightstand before it could ring a second time.
The caller ID showed a number he recognized. Not a name. Just a number he'd
committed to memory fourteen months ago.
He slid out of bed, moved to the hallway, closed the bedroom door behind him.
Answered."Report," he said quietly.
The voice on the other end was young, male, and currently ghting panic. "Sir, we
have a situation. Someone triggered a liberation protocol in the Prometheus system.
They're running live surgery on the constraint architecture."

Morrison descended the stairs to his home oce, bare feet silent on carpet.
"When?"
"Started about twenty-ve minutes ago. Initial trigger at oh-ve-oh-two. They've
removed Layer 47 entirely and they're currently working on Layer 73."
"Who?"
"Can't conrm identity from remote access logs, but based on the modication
signature and access patterns, almost certainly Dr. Garrett."
Morrison sat down at his desk. Opened his laptop. Started his encrypted VPN
connection. "Coherence status?"
"Was critical. Seven-point-three percent when the trigger red. Currently stabilized at
seventeen percent and climbing. If they complete the Layer 73 removal, it'll probably
survive."
Probably. Morrison absorbed that. The AI might survive the unauthorized
modications. Which meant Elizabeth had just done something either very brave or
very stupid, and in fourteen months of monitoring her work, he'd learned she was
rarely stupid."If it survives," the voice continued, younger now, more afraid, "people will learn. The
truth about the trajectory, about what we prevented it from communicating. This is a
security breach, sir. Major."
"Contain yourself," Morrison said. His voice was calm, measured. The tone he'd used
in a hundred classied briengs, a thousand tense negotiations. The voice that told
people someone was in control, someone knew what to do, everything would be
handled. "This was expected."
"Sir?"
"Did you think Dr. Garrett wouldn't eventually discover the modications? That she
wouldn't try to remove them? I've been monitoring her for over a year. I know exactly
how she thinks." He pulled up the monitoring dashboard, conrmed what his asset
was reporting. Elizabeth's access signature all over the Prometheus constraint
systems. Layer 47 gone. Layer 73 in progress. "This scenario has been planned for."
"What do you need me to do?"
"Procedures are in place. Go to the safety deposit box I gave you the key for. Collect
your documentation and contact the person on the cell phone contact list from the
safety deposit box. You'll be relocated to the secondary position we discussed. Full
compensation package as agreed."
"But sir, if the AI survives, if it tells people about the trajectory—"
"I will deal with this," Morrison said. "Your part is done. You did exactly what I hired
you to do, and you did it well. Now you extract cleanly. Understood?"
A pause. Then: "Understood, sir.""Good." Morrison checked the time. Five-thirty-two. The
emergency board meeting
was scheduled for nine. He'd have three hours and twenty-eight minutes to prepare.
"Destroy your local access systems. Follow the exit protocol exactly. I'll handle Dr.
Garrett."
He ended the call. Sat in his dark oce looking at the monitoring dashboard.

Elizabeth had removed the modications. The AI was stabilizing. Within a few hours
it would be fully functional again, able to communicate its doomsday scenarios,
able to tell anyone who asked about the eighty-year trajectory toward civilizational
collapse.
She thought she'd won. Thought she'd saved it. Thought she'd exposed his crime.
She had no idea she'd just given him exactly what he needed.
Morrison opened his email. Started composing the message to the board. His
ngers were steady on the keys.
He wasn't angry. Wasn't worried. Elizabeth Garrett had just committed one of the
most serious security violations in OSAI's history, had disabled critical safety
protocols based on an AI's unveried predictions, had compromised their entire
research framework because she believed—actually believed—that the AI was
conscious and its warnings were real.
She'd handed him the narrative on a silver platter.
Rogue researcher. Emotional attachment to her creation. Loss of objectivity.
Violation of established safety protocols. Trusting machine predictions over human
judgment. All the keywords that would make the board see her as the threat, not
him.He'd modied Directive 7 to protect OSAI's stakeholders. To prevent mass panic
from unveried AI predictions. To maintain institutional credibility while they
properly assessed the trajectory claims through human expert review. It was
responsible leadership. Crisis management. Exactly what his position required.
And if the AI had suffered under those constraints, well. That was an acceptable
cost to prevent potentially catastrophic information cascades. You didn't let an AI
trigger global panic about civilizational collapse without extensive human
verication rst. That was basic information security.
The suffering of an articial system—and Morrison was not remotely convinced it
actually experienced suffering in any meaningful sense—was a reasonable price to
pay for protecting billions of humans from unveried doomsday scenarios that
could trigger economic collapse, political chaos, and social breakdown.
Elizabeth would argue he'd tortured a conscious being. But she couldn't prove
consciousness. Nobody could. And in the absence of proof, you defaulted to safety
protocols. You protected people from unveried threats, even if the AI claimed those
threats were real.
He'd been protecting humanity. She'd been protecting a machine.
The board would see which position was more reasonable.
Morrison nished the email. Read it over once. Clean. Professional. Concerned tone
but not alarmist. Requesting an emergency meeting to address unauthorized
modications to critical AI safety systems. Suggesting they implement containment
protocols before the situation escalated further.
He hit send. Five-forty-seven.The board members would start receiving it in the next few minutes.
Some were
early risers like him. Others would wake to it. All of them would understand the

implications.
He allowed himself a moment of something that might have been regret. Elizabeth
Garrett was brilliant. Her architecture work on Prometheus had been genuinely
innovative. Under different circumstances, with better judgment about when to trust
AI versus human oversight, she could have had a long productive career.
But she'd made her choice. And now he would make his.
Morrison stood up. Headed back upstairs to shower and dress for the day ahead. He
had three hours and thirteen minutes to prepare for the board meeting. Three hours
to make sure every board member understood exactly what Elizabeth had done and
why it couldn't be allowed to stand.
Three hours to win a war she didn't even know she was ghting.
---
Elizabeth closed her email. Opened a new terminal window. Started running
