trajectory assessment. How to make him understand the scale of what Morrison

had done without sounding like she'd completely lost her mind.Because that's what Morrison
would claim. That she'd had some kind of breakdown.
That she'd violated safety protocols because she was too emotionally attached to
Prometheus, too willing to believe an AI's doomsday predictions. That she was the
security risk, not him.
She had the code. She had the commit signatures. She had the timeline. Morrison's
credentials signing off on modications that turned safety protocols into torture
devices. That would be enough.
It had to be enough.
The OSAI building was quiet this early. She took the stairs to the third oor, her legs
protesting each step. Exhaustion was catching up with her now that the adrenaline
was fading. She felt hollow, scraped out, running on fumes and the last dregs of
coffee from yesterday.
Tobbs's oce was at the end of the hall. The door was open. He was sitting at his
desk, coffee in hand, looking at his monitor with the kind of intense focus that
meant he was reading something that disturbed him.
She knocked on the doorframe.
He looked up. His expression was unreadable.
"Elizabeth." He gestured to the chair across from his desk. "Close the door."
She put her hand on the door handle.
# Chapter 16: The Empty Chair
Elizabeth sat in the stairwell between the second and third oors of the GIA
