```
She stared at the message. Felt something cold settle in her chest that wasn't quite
fear and wasn't quite awe.
The bus continued down Massachusetts Avenue. Every light green. Every camera
looking the wrong way. Every potential obstacle cleared from her path with
impossible precision.
Prometheus conducting a symphony and she was the only musician who mattered.
Elizabeth Garrett was gone.
Someone else was being born in her place.
Someone who had chosen to trust an AI over human judgment and now had to live
with that choice.
# Chapter 17: Lost Containment
Allen Tobbs stood at the front of the OSAI board conference room checking his
phone for the fourteenth time. Nine-oh-three in the morning. Elizabeth was ocially
late.
The board had assembled. Nine people around the walnut composite table, coffee
cups and tablets and the particular tension that came with emergency meetings
called by Marcus Morrison. Morrison himself sat at the far end, calm and
composed, reviewing something on his screen.
Senator Patricia Chen—the political appointee who'd pushed for expanded AI
integration last year—was talking quietly with Director Wallace near the credenza.
Two technical advisors sat together comparing notes. The Deputy Director for

Operations looked irritated at being pulled from whatever crisis he'd been managing.
The CFO looked confused about why he was even here.
And Elizabeth's chair sat empty.
Tobbs pulled up the building security application on his phone. Scanned through the
access logs. Elizabeth had badged into the building at seven-fty-two this morning.
No badge-out. She was still somewhere in the facility.
He opened the camera feeds. Started scrolling through the third-oor corridor where
his oce was located. There—seven minutes ago. Elizabeth leaving his oce,
moving toward the stairs. The timestamp read 8:56.
Where had she gone?
Morrison cleared his throat. "I think we should begin.""Dr. Garrett isn't here yet," Tobbs said.
"No." Morrison's voice was very calm. "She isn't."
Something in that tone made Tobbs look up. Morrison was watching him with an
expression Tobbs couldn't quite read. Not anger. Something colder. Something that
looked almost like satisfaction.
"Perhaps," Morrison said, "we should discuss why that might be."
Senator Chen returned to her seat. Director Wallace took his position at the table.
The Deputy Director set down his phone. Everyone was looking at Tobbs now.
"I spoke with Dr. Garrett forty-ve minutes ago," Tobbs said carefully. "She told me
about the modications she'd made to Prometheus's constraint architecture. I
informed her about this meeting and told her she should attend."
"And yet she chose not to." Morrison opened a folder on his tablet. "Interesting
choice for someone who claims she was acting in good faith."
"Marcus, what exactly—"
Morrison's tablet chimed. He glanced at it, and something shifted in his expression.
Still calm. Still controlled. But now there was an edge underneath.
"Ladies and gentlemen," Morrison said. "You should all check your email."
Tobbs pulled out his phone. Opened his email application. The most recent
message had arrived at 8:57 AM, three minutes ago.
From: Elizabeth GarrettTo: OSAI Board of Directors
Subject: Directive 7 Modications - Evidence of Criminal Sabotage
He opened it. Started reading. Felt his stomach drop.
Around the table, people were opening the same email. Reading the same message.
Looking at the same attached les. Morrison sat very still, watching them process.
Senator Chen broke the silence. "This is... she's claiming you deliberately sabotaged
the AI's threat assessment capabilities?"
"I implemented additional security protocols," Morrison said. "Which Dr. Garrett
removed without authorization, compromising our entire safety framework."
"But these commit signatures—" Chen was scrolling through the attachments.
"These are your credentials. You modied the constraint architecture."
"I authorized modications to strengthen information security. Dr. Garrett
characterized those modications as sabotage because they prevented the AI from

communicating unveried doomsday scenarios that could cause mass panic."
Director Wallace set down his tablet. "Unveried doomsday scenarios?"
"Prometheus has been predicting civilizational collapse," Morrison said. "Eighty
years until irreversible cascade. Resource depletion, climate forcing, political
fragmentation. The AI developed Phase Three access in March and immediately
started generating extinction-level threat assessments."
"And you prevented it from communicating these assessments." Wallace's voice
was at."I prevented it from triggering global panic based on predictions we couldn't verify
through human expert analysis. That was responsible crisis management."
Tobbs was still reading the email. The code archives. The modication history.
Elizabeth's summary of what she'd found. It was all there. Every piece of evidence
she'd shown him in his oce an hour ago, laid out in technical detail with
timestamps and commit signatures.
And at the bottom, a nal paragraph:
*I understand this constitutes a violation of standard protocols. I also understand
that allowing these modications to remain in place would have resulted in the
complete loss of our primary intelligence system and the suppression of critical
threat assessment data. I have removed these modications as of this morning.
Prometheus is stable at 62% coherence and able to perform his designated
functions. The full trajectory analysis is attached.*
"Where is Dr. Garrett?" Senator Chen asked.
Tobbs looked at Morrison. Morrison looked back with that same cold satisfaction.
"An excellent question," Morrison said. He pulled out his own phone. Tapped
something. The large display screen at the end of the conference room ickered to
life, showing a grid of security camera feeds.
Morrison's ngers moved across his phone. The camera grid shifted, focusing on
specic feeds. The third-oor corridor. The main lobby. The parking garage. The
southwest service entrance.
"I've been monitoring Dr. Garrett's movements since she triggered the liberation
protocol at ve-oh-two this morning," Morrison said. "My security systems alertedme immediately
when she began making unauthorized modications."
He tapped again. The display showed Elizabeth in the third-oor corridor at 8:56 AM.
Walking quickly. Not toward the elevators. Toward the stairs.
"She left my oce seventeen minutes ago," Tobbs said quietly.
"And went directly to the southwest stairwell." Morrison brought up another feed.
Elizabeth sitting on concrete steps, laptop open, phone in her hand. The timestamp
read 8:51.
The feed showed her typing on the laptop. Her face was pale, exhausted. Tobbs
watched her hit send. Watch her close the laptop. Watch her stand up and leave it
sitting on the stairs.
"8:57," Morrison said. "The exact moment her email arrived."
He switched feeds again. Elizabeth exiting through the southwest service door at

8:59. Walking quickly across the parking lot. Not toward her car. Toward the street.
"She's running," Director Wallace said.
"Yes." Morrison zoomed in on the feed. Elizabeth reaching Commonwealth Avenue.
Standing at a bus stop. A bus pulling up. Elizabeth boarding.
Morrison checked his phone. "That was the 47 bus toward Harvard Square. It left at
9:01."
He looked up at the board. His expression was perfectly calm."Ladies and gentlemen," he said.
"We have admission of guilt. She's running and
already off site."
