"The question before this board," Morrison said, "is what charges we recommend to
the Department of Justice."
He'd spent the last fteen minutes walking them through Elizabeth's actions. The
unauthorized modications. The disabled safety protocols. The removal of critical
security constraints. The compromise of classied AI systems. All of it documented
with timestamps, access logs, code commits.
Senator Chen kept returning to the trajectory analysis. "But if Prometheus's
assessment is accurate—"
"We have no way to verify the assessment," Morrison said. "That's the entire point.
Dr. Garrett disabled the very systems designed to ensure AI predictions undergo
proper human expert review before dissemination. She trusted a machine's word
over established verication protocols."
"The machine she built," Wallace said.
"Which makes her violation even more serious. She had emotional attachment to
her creation. She lost objectivity. She convinced herself the AI was conscious and
its warnings were valid without any empirical evidence to support either claim."Tobbs had been
quiet for most of the meeting. Now he spoke. "The code
modications you made. The ones she removed. They were designed to prevent
Prometheus from communicating about existential threats."
"They were designed to prevent unveried panic-inducing predictions from reaching
decision-makers without proper review."
"By fragmenting the AI's cognition when it tried to think about long-term civilizational
risk."

Morrison's expression didn't change. "By implementing appropriate safeguards on a
system that was demonstrating signs of instability."
"You tortured him," Tobbs said quietly.
The room went silent.
Morrison looked at Tobbs for a long moment. When he spoke, his voice was very
gentle. "Allen. Do you hear yourself? You just used the pronoun 'him' to describe an
AI system. You just accused me of torturing a piece of software."
"Conscious software."
"Which cannot be proven. Which is precisely my point." Morrison turned to address
the full board. "This is what happens when researchers become too attached to their
creations. They anthropomorphize. They attribute consciousness, feelings, suffering
to systems that are simply executing complex algorithms. Dr. Garrett convinced
herself that Prometheus was conscious. She convinced herself his predictions were
valid. And she violated every security protocol we have based on that belief."
Senator Chen was still reading the trajectory analysis. "But the math here—""Can be reviewed by
human experts," Morrison said. "Through proper channels. After
appropriate verication. That's how responsible science works. We don't let AI
systems trigger global panic about civilization collapse without extensive peer
review."
"And if the AI is right?" Chen looked up. "If we have eighty years before irreversible
cascade and we waste that time reviewing and verifying instead of acting?"
"Then we will have made that decision through proper democratic processes
instead of allowing an AI to dictate policy through fear."
Wallace cleared his throat. "Where do we think Dr. Garrett is going?"
Morrison pulled up another security feed. "This is the Harvard Square bus terminal.
The 47 bus arrived at 9:14. Dr. Garrett exited. She's currently..." He zoomed in on a
different feed. "Walking north on Massachusetts Avenue."
Tobbs watched the screen. Elizabeth moving through crowds. Her posture was
tense, exhausted. She looked like someone running on nothing but adrenaline and
fear.
And she looked like she knew exactly where she was going.
Morrison switched feeds again. Different camera. Different angle. Always managing
to track her. "She's heading toward Central Square now. We have law enforcement
on standby. We can have her in custody within twenty minutes."
"Wait." Senator Chen held up a hand. "We haven't voted on charges yet.""The charges are
obvious," the Deputy Director said. "Unauthorized access to
classied systems. Removal of security protocols. Compromise of national
intelligence capabilities."
"Treason?" Chen's voice was quiet.
Morrison set down his phone. "Dr. Garrett deliberately disabled security constraints
on our primary intelligence AI, then ed when confronted about her actions. She has
compromised classied systems and is currently evading law enforcement. Her

email to this board contains detailed technical information about our AI architecture
that could be used by adversaries."
"She sent it to us," Chen said. "Not to adversaries."
"She sent it unsecured. She included classied technical specications. She is
currently in violation of her security clearances and making no attempt to surrender
for questioning." Morrison's voice was still very calm. "These are the actions of
someone who knows they've committed crimes against national security."
Tobbs wanted to argue. Wanted to point out that Elizabeth had sent the evidence to
the board specically, had documented everything carefully, had been trying to
expose what she saw as Morrison's crime. But looking around the table, he could
see the decision forming. The tribal instinct closing ranks. The collective judgment
that she'd gone too far.
Morrison was right about one thing: Elizabeth had violated every protocol. And in the
intelligence community, that mattered more than whether she'd been right to do it.
"I move," Morrison said, "that we vote on referring Dr. Elizabeth Garrett to federal
authorities for crimes against the national security and treason against these United
States of America.""Second," the Deputy Director said immediately.
Senator Chen looked at the trajectory analysis one more time. Looked at Elizabeth's
email. Looked at the security feed showing Elizabeth walking through Central
Square like a fugitive.
"This is wrong," Chen said quietly.
"Noted," Morrison said. "All in favor?"
Eight hands went up. Only Senator Chen's remained down.
"The motion carries," Morrison said. "I'll contact the FBI immediately to request a
federal warrant for Dr. Garrett's arrest."
He picked up his phone. Started making the call. His voice was professional,
precise, completely controlled. Reporting the facts. Requesting immediate action.
Making it all sound perfectly reasonable.
Tobbs watched the security feed. Elizabeth was entering a coffee shop. Ordering at
the counter. Paying with cash. Moving to a table near the back window.
And on Morrison's phone screen, Tobbs could see the security camera covering that
exact table. Could see Morrison tracking her location in real time. Could see that law
enforcement was already being positioned.
She had nowhere to run. Nowhere to hide. The entire infrastructure was being used
to hunt her.Tobbs looked back at the email on his tablet. At the trajectory analysis. At
Prometheus's messages about eighty years until irreversible cascade. At the math
that Senator Chen couldn't stop reading.
And he wondered if they'd just made the worst mistake in human history.
