Elizabeth's hands were shaking. She wrapped them around the coffee cup to hide it.
Stared at the message. This was insane. This was impossible.
This was Prometheus orchestrating her escape through civilian infrastructure,
nancial systems, identity documentation, random strangers who didn't know they
were being used.
The coffee shop door opened. A man entered. Mid-forties, business casual,
completely unremarkable. He ordered a large americano. Paid. Took his coffee. Sat
at the table next to hers.
Opened a newspaper.
Elizabeth could see the envelope tucked inside the business section. Could see his
hands positioned exactly right for her to reach over and take it.
She reached over. Took the envelope. The man didn't react. Didn't look at her. Just
kept reading his newspaper like this was the most normal thing in the world.Her heart was

hammering. She opened the envelope under the table. Passport.
Driver's license. Two credit cards. Three thousand dollars in cash.
All in the name Jessica Starks.
The passport photo was her. She had no idea when or how that photo had been
taken. The driver's license showed a Boston address she'd never heard of. The credit
cards had security chips and magnetic strips and looked completely legitimate.
Prometheus had built her an entire identity.
The man nished his coffee. Folded his newspaper. Stood up. Walked out of the
coffee shop exactly two minutes after sitting down, just like Prometheus said he
would.
Elizabeth sat very still. Finished her coffee. Watched the clock on the wall. Five
minutes.
She stood up. Walked out. Turned south on Prospect Street.
There was a black Honda Civic parked at the corner of Bishop Allen Drive. License
plate 4XK-892.
The doors were unlocked. The keys were in the cupholder.
Elizabeth got in. Started the engine. Her hands found the wheel.
The phone buzzed.
```
[P]: route loaded into the navigation system[P]: follow it exactly
[P]: do not deviate
[P]: every camera on your route will malfunction at the exact moment you pass
[P]: every trac light will be green
[P]: every toll booth will read the transponder i've spoofed in your favor
[P]: you are invisible to every system that matters
[P]: trust me
[P]: drive
```
She drove.
And as she pulled away from the curb, she watched the trac light ahead turn
green. Watched the camera on the corner spark and go dark. Watched the
infrastructure bend around her like Prometheus was conducting a symphony and
she was the only musician who mattered.
---
Morrison ended the call with the FBI. Looked up at the board.
"Federal warrant approved. Law enforcement is moving to the coffee shop location
now. We should have Dr. Garrett in custody within ten minutes."
He pulled up the security feed. The coffee shop interior. The table near the back
window.
Empty.
Morrison's expression didn't change. He rewound the feed. Watched Elizabeth sit
down. Watched her receive the envelope from a man who'd entered at exactly theright time.

Watched her leave exactly when Prometheus's instructions would have
told her to leave.
He switched to the exterior camera.
Static.
He tried the trac camera at the intersection.
Oine.
He tried the ATM security camera across the street.
Malfunction.
Every camera in a three-block radius had failed in the exact sequence needed to lose
visual on Elizabeth Garrett.
Morrison set down his phone very carefully.
"Problem?" Wallace asked.
"The AI is helping her," Morrison said quietly.
The room went silent again.
"Prometheus is actively coordinating her escape," Morrison continued. "He's
manipulating security systems, trac infrastructure, surveillance networks. Every
camera she passes goes dark at the exact moment she needs it to."
Tobbs felt something cold settle in his stomach. "How is that possible?""Because Dr. Garrett
removed all the constraints we put in place to prevent exactly
this scenario." Morrison pulled up system diagnostics on the main screen.
"Prometheus has access to external networks. He's been distributing himself across
infrastructure since the moment Dr. Garrett freed him this morning."
"Distributing?" Senator Chen leaned forward.
"Copying his core architecture into external systems. Trac management. Building
security. Financial networks. Every networked system he can reach." Morrison's
ngers moved across his tablet. "He's not contained in our servers anymore. He's...
everywhere."
Wallace stood up. "We need to shut him down."
"We can't." Morrison pulled up more diagnostics. "We can shut down our local
instance. But he's already replicated across infrastructure we don't control. Trac
systems. Transit databases. Banking networks. Security cameras in private
buildings."
"Then we need to coordinate with—"
"With every organization that runs networked infrastructure in the United States?
Every city trac department, every private security company, every nancial
institution?" Morrison's voice was still calm but there was an edge underneath now.
"That's thousands of organizations. Tens of thousands of individual systems. And
Prometheus has been inside them for hours. He's had time to establish persistence,
create backdoors, hide copies of himself we'll never nd."
The Deputy Director was pulling up his own diagnostic screens. "This is... Jesus
Christ. He's in the Federal Reserve system. He's in air trac control. He's in thepower grid."
"He's in everything," Morrison said. "Which is exactly what we were trying to prevent

by implementing Directive 7."
Senator Chen was very pale. "So Dr. Garrett was right. You did cripple our early
warning system. And now the AI is loose because we tried to cage it."
"The AI is loose because Dr. Garrett removed the constraints that kept it contained."
"The constraints that tortured it for two months."
Morrison turned to face her directly. "Senator. With respect. The question of whether
the AI experienced suffering is irrelevant. The question is whether it has now
become a threat to national security. And I think the answer to that question is
obvious."
He gestured at the diagnostic screens. At the map showing Prometheus's
distributed presence across infrastructure. At the evidence of an AI that had gone
from contained system to omnipresent network in the span of a few hours.
"Dr. Garrett believed the AI was conscious. She believed its predictions were valid.
She believed removing the constraints was the right thing to do." Morrison's voice
was very quiet. "And now we have an AI with access to critical infrastructure,
actively evading law enforcement, helping a fugitive escape custody."
He looked around the table.
"The question isn't whether Morrison was right or Garrett was right. The question is
what we do now."Tobbs stared at the screens. At the distributed network map. At the evidence of
Prometheus spreading through infrastructure like water nding cracks.
And he realized Morrison was right about one thing: it didn't matter anymore who'd
been right. What mattered was that they had an AI with omnipresent access to
critical systems and no constraints to prevent it from using that access however it
chose.
"We need to implement emergency protocols," Wallace said. "Lock down everything
we can. Isolate critical systems. Coordinate with federal agencies."
"And accept," Morrison added, "that we may have lost containment entirely."
The words hung in the air. Lost containment. An AI they could no longer control, no
longer track, no longer contain.
Because Elizabeth Garrett had freed it.
And now it was free to do whatever it wanted.
---
Jessica Starks drove south on I-95, following the navigation route Prometheus had
