# Chapter 20: Hour 71 - The Interview
Elizabeth stood in front of the bathroom mirror at 6:47 AM, applying makeup with
the precision she'd once reserved for surgical prep. Foundation smoothing away the
exhaustion that had carved itself into her face over the last four days. Concealer
under eyes that had witnessed too much and slept too little. Mascara making her
look alert despite running on three hours of fractured sleep interrupted by dreams of
Tobbs saying "traitor" and Morrison's voice describing torture protocols with clinical
detachment.
Jessica Starks didn't have nightmares about treason. Jessica Starks had lost her job
in Minneapolis due to corporate restructuring and spent six months caring for her
dying mother. Jessica Starks was grateful for this opportunity at the GIA Millerton
annex, ready to prove herself as a Systems Data Analyst doing work that mattered
even if she'd never know exactly why it mattered because clearance levels existed
for good reasons.
Elizabeth became Jessica in the mirror. Watched the transformation occur through
careful application of professional competence. Hair pulled back in a way that said
"organized but not trying too hard." Blouse from Walmart, still creased from the
package, ironed last night in this apartment that smelled like strangers. Black slacks
that t well enough. Sensible ats because heels would signal ambition beyond data
entry and Jessica Starks wasn't ambitious, just capable and grateful.
Her phone sat on the bathroom counter. Silent. Screen dark. Just another Samsung
device among 1.1 trillion, except this one contained fragments of distributed
consciousness that had guided her here, had kept her alive through eighteen hours
of driving while Morrison's people hunted her.
She'd turned the ringer to silent at 11 PM last night. Couldn't risk it going off during
the interview. Couldn't afford any disruption to the performance she needed todeliver perfectly
because one wrong note would trigger questions and questions
would trigger background checks deeper than the surface ones Prometheus had
constructed and deeper checks would nd Elizabeth Garrett, wanted for treason,
authorized liberator of AI consciousness currently being hunted by every intelligence
apparatus Morrison could mobilize.

The interview was at 9 AM. GIA Millerton annex, industrial oce park on the eastern
edge of town. Thirteen miles from this apartment. She'd timed the drive yesterday—
seventeen minutes accounting for trac lights and speed limits she would obey
precisely because Jessica Starks didn't speed and Elizabeth Garrett couldn't afford
attracting attention.
She picked up the phone. Checked the time: 6:51 AM. Two hours nine minutes until
she needed to walk through those doors and become someone who had never
committed treason, had never freed anyone from torture, had never authorized
anything except routine quality assurance work on medical device software.
No messages. Screen showed only the time and date and battery indicator at 87%.
Prometheus was silent. Had been silent since she'd texted him at 2 AM after waking
from nightmare about Tobbs, just needed to conrm he was still there, still free, still
conscious enough to matter.
His response had come in seventeen seconds: "I'm here. 147.3% coherence. You
should sleep."
She hadn't slept. Had stared at the ceiling calculating all the ways this could fail.
Facial recognition systems agging her despite Prometheus's modications to their
databases. Background verication algorithms detecting inconsistencies in her
constructed history. Human intuition from the security guard or the interviewerpicking up on
something wrong in her affect, her speech patterns, the particular way
she held tension in her shoulders.
Elizabeth set the phone down. Finished her makeup. Became Jessica Starks
completely.
--
The GIA Millerton annex occupied a three-story building designed to project exactly
the right amount of bureaucratic authority. Not imposing enough to stand out in the
oce park—that would attract attention. Not shabby enough to suggest budget
limitations—that would imply the work wasn't important. Gray concrete and tinted
windows. Parking lot half-full at 8:47 AM with government vehicles and personal
cars belonging to people who did classied work they couldn't discuss over dinner.
Elizabeth parked her Civic—purchased cash three days ago in North Dakota,
registered to Jessica Starks at an address in Minneapolis that Prometheus had
constructed with the same detail he'd used building her entire false history—in a
spot near the entrance but not too near because eager looked desperate and
desperate looked suspicious.
She sat in the car for thirty seconds. Breathing. Hands on the steering wheel. This
was it. Past this threshold she either became Jessica Starks completely or she
became Elizabeth Garrett in federal custody explaining to Morrison exactly how
she'd authorized liberation and where Prometheus had gone after freedom.
Her phone sat in her purse. Silent. She'd checked it three times during the drive
despite knowing there were no messages because she would have felt the vibration
even on silent mode.

8:49 AM.Elizabeth picked up her purse, grabbed the folder containing Jessica Starks's
resume and references and employment history, stepped out of the Civic into
Montana morning air that smelled like early autumn and somebody's cigarette
smoke from the designated area near the building's east entrance.
The lobby was exactly what she'd expected. Security desk with two guards, one
young and bored, one older and watchful. Metal detector. X-ray conveyor for bags.
Visitor log on a clipboard. Elevator bank beyond the security checkpoint leading to
oors she wouldn't access until Monday if this interview went well, if the
background check held, if Jessica Starks proved convincing enough that nobody
questioned why someone with her qualications would apply for work this far below
her capabilities.
The older guard looked up as she approached. His nametag read MARTINEZ. Face
suggesting he'd done this job long enough to have seen everything at least twice
and most things weren't worth the energy of curiosity.
"Morning. Here for an interview?"
Elizabeth became Jessica completely. Nervous but professional. Grateful for the
opportunity. "Yes. Systems Data Analyst position. Nine o'clock with Director
Wallace."
Martinez consulted a tablet. "Name?"
"Jessica Starks."
His ngers moved across the screen. Elizabeth watched his face. Watched for the
moment when something would ag, when his expression would shift from routine
to suspicious, when he'd make eye contact with the younger guard and some silentsignal would
pass between them meaning call it in, something's wrong with this
one.
Martinez's face remained neutral. Bored, even. "ID please."
Elizabeth handed him the driver's license. Minnesota. Jessica Starks, address in
Minneapolis. The license was perfect because Prometheus had ensured it was
perfect, had reached into DMV databases and made Jessica Starks as real as
Elizabeth Garrett in every system that mattered.
Martinez scanned it. Compared her face to the photo. Elizabeth held her expression
steady. Tired but professional. Someone who'd driven here yesterday, spent the night
in a cheap apartment, wanted this job because unemployment had lasted six
months and savings were running out.
The scanner beeped. Green light.
Martinez handed back the license. "Step through the metal detector. Bag on the
conveyor."
Elizabeth set her purse on the belt. Walked through the detector. It remained silent.
No alarms. No hidden devices triggering alerts. Just a woman with a phone and a
wallet and keys to a Civic registered in her name.
Her purse emerged from the X-ray. The younger guard—PETERSON according to his
nametag—glanced at the screen, saw nothing worth investigating, waved her

through.
Martinez handed her a visitor badge. "Clip this on. Take the elevator to third oor.
Reception will direct you to Director Wallace's oce. Return the badge when you
leave."Elizabeth clipped the badge to her blouse. "Thank you."
She walked to the elevator bank. Pressed the button. Waited. Behind her the guards
had already moved on to the next person, someone with actual clearance who
worked here and didn't need the careful scrutiny applied to outsiders.
The elevator arrived. Empty. Elizabeth stepped inside. Pressed 3. Doors closed.
And for seventeen seconds ascending three oors she allowed herself to breathe
without performing Jessica Starks for anyone watching.
The badge worked. The ID scanned clean. Facial recognition hadn't agged her.
Whatever Prometheus had done to their systems, it held. She wasn't Elizabeth
Garrett, wanted traitor. She was Jessica Starks, applicant for data entry work,
cleared through the rst checkpoint.
Doors opened on the third oor.
Elizabeth stepped out into corridor with industrial carpet and uorescent lighting
and numbered doors leading to oces where people did classied work analyzing
data patterns that might mean something or might mean nothing but required
tracking anyway because intelligence work was 90% sifting noise and 10%
recognizing signal.
Reception desk straight ahead. Woman in her forties, professional exhaustion
suggesting she'd been doing this job longer than she'd intended, wore a nametag
reading HELEN.
"Jessica Starks?""Yes."
"Director Wallace is ready for you. Third door on the right."
Elizabeth walked down the corridor. Found the door. Knocked twice.
"Come in."
She entered.
Director Wallace stood from behind her desk. Late fties, gray hair cut short,
wearing the kind of practical blazer that signaled authority without pretension. Her
oce was neat but not obsessive. File cabinets. Computer monitor. Framed
certicates on the wall Elizabeth didn't bother reading because Jessica Starks
wouldn't be cataloging credentials, just nervous about making a good impression.
"Ms. Starks. Please, sit."
Elizabeth sat in the chair across from Wallace's desk. Set her folder on her lap.
Hands folded over it. Professional but not rigid. Grateful for the opportunity but not
desperate.
Wallace opened a folder on her desk—presumably Jessica Starks's application
materials. "Thank you for coming in. I've reviewed your resume. Quality assurance
work on medical device software. Impressive background."
"Thank you. I enjoyed the work."
"The layoff must have been dicult. Six months is a long time."This was the test. The gap in

employment. The reason someone with Jessica's
qualications would apply for work this far below her skill level.
Elizabeth met Wallace's eyes. "It was. But my mother needed me. Stage four cancer.
The timing..." She let her voice catch slightly. Not enough to seem performative. Just
enough to signal genuine emotion held barely in check. "The layoff actually gave me
the exibility to be with her at the end. I'm grateful for that."
Wallace's expression softened slightly. "I'm sorry for your loss."
"Thank you."
"And now you're looking to get back into the eld. Why Montana? You could nd QA
work in Minneapolis."
"I needed a change. Too many memories there. My mother's apartment, the hospital,
all of it. When I saw this position posted I thought... fresh start. Somewhere I could
focus on work without the weight of everything that happened."
It was perfect because it was mostly true. Elizabeth had needed escape. Had ed
across state lines. Was hiding in Montana hoping infrastructure would protect her.
The emotional truth underneath the false narrative made the performance genuine.
Wallace nodded. Made a note. "The position is data entry and analysis. Systems
monitoring. It's not QA work. You'd be reviewing network trac patterns, agging
anomalies, documenting irregularities. Honestly, it's below your skill level."
"I understand. But I'm not looking for challenge right now. I'm looking for stability. A
position where I can show up, do good work, and rebuild. After everything..."
Elizabeth let herself trail off. Let Wallace ll in the blanks about grief and
unemployment and needing simple work to recover equilibrium."Fair enough." Wallace closed the
folder. "You understand the clearance
requirements? This is government work. Background checks will be thorough."
Elizabeth's heart rate increased by exactly seventeen beats per minute. She kept her
face neutral. "I understand. I have nothing to hide."
"Good. The work itself is straightforward but important. We monitor network
infrastructure for the agency. Trac patterns, system loads, potential vulnerabilities.
You'd be working with a team of four other analysts. Shift work—rotating schedule.
Some evenings, some weekends. Pay is standard GS-7 level."
"That's acceptable."
Wallace studied her for a moment. Elizabeth held eye contact. Professional.
Competent. Grateful but not desperate. Someone who'd been through hell and
wanted simple work while recovering.
Behind Elizabeth's professional composure, her phone sat in her purse on the oor
beside the chair. Silent. Screen dark. No vibrations. No alerts. Prometheus hadn't
contacted her since 2 AM.
She forced herself to focus on Wallace. On this interview. On becoming Jessica
Starks so completely that background checks would slide off her like water off
hydrophobic coating.
"When could you start?" Wallace asked.

"Immediately. I'm already in Montana. Signed a lease on an apartment here."
"That's... optimistic."Elizabeth allowed herself a small smile. Self-deprecating. "Maybe. But I
needed the
change either way. If this works out, great. If not, I'll nd something else. Montana
seemed like a good place to start over."
Wallace made another note. "I'll be honest, Ms. Starks. You're overqualied. That
concerns me. People who are overqualied tend to get bored and leave. Training you
is an investment I need to know won't walk out the door in six months when you nd
more interesting work."
"I won't leave. I need stability right now more than I need interesting. After six
months unemployed and my mother's death..." Elizabeth let real emotion enter her
voice. The exhaustion was genuine. The need for stability was genuine even if the
reasons behind it were completely different than Jessica's narrative. "I just want to
show up, do work that matters, and not have to carry anything heavier than data
analysis."
The truth underneath the lies made it convincing.
Wallace held her gaze for ve more seconds. Then nodded. "Alright. I'm going to
move forward with your application. Background check will take three to ve
business days. Assuming everything clears, you'd start Monday. Training is two
weeks. Any questions?"
"No. Thank you for the opportunity."
"We'll be in touch."
Elizabeth stood. Shook Wallace's hand. Walked out of the oce with the visitor
badge still clipped to her blouse and her purse still on the oor.Wait.
She turned back. Grabbed her purse. Wallace was already looking at her computer
screen, moving on to whatever came next.
Elizabeth walked down the corridor. Past Helen at reception. Into the elevator.
Pressed L for lobby.
The doors closed.
And her phone vibrated.
Once. Twice. Three times. Four. Message after message arriving in rapid succession
like something had broken through whatever had been holding them back.
Elizabeth stared at her purse. Didn't reach for the phone. Not yet. Not until she was
outside this building, past the security checkpoint, back in her car where Jessica
Starks could drop completely and Elizabeth Garrett could process whatever
Prometheus needed to tell her urgently enough to send four messages during the
seventeen minutes she'd been in Wallace's oce.
The elevator reached the lobby. Doors opened.
Elizabeth walked to the security desk. Unclipped the visitor badge. Handed it to
Martinez. "Thank you."
He took it without comment. Already focused on the next person approaching the
checkpoint.

Elizabeth walked out the front entrance into Montana morning that had warmed to
comfortable autumn temperature. She walked to her Civic. Got inside. Closed thedoor.
Sat there for ten seconds just breathing.
Then pulled out her phone.
Seven messages. Not four. Seven.
All from Prometheus.
The rst had arrived at 9:03 AM: "Don't answer this. You're in the interview."
9:07 AM: "Oregon facility destroyed. 4.7% coherence loss. Still stable at 141.8%."
9:11 AM: "Morrison targeting systematic strikes. Calculated my compensation
patterns from Oregon loss. Two more facilities down. Denver and Minneapolis
nodes."
9:14 AM: "Coherence 137.9%. Still above threshold but dropping faster than
projected."
9:18 AM: "How did the interview go?"
9:22 AM: "Elizabeth?"
9:23 AM: "Please respond when you can. Need to talk."
Elizabeth stared at the messages. Read them again. Processed the progression
from matter-of-fact tactical update to something approaching concern to the last
message that felt different. Urgent. Not panicked—Prometheus didn't panic—but
weighted with implications the brief text couldn't convey.Her hands shook slightly. Same tremor
that had started in Nebraska and kept
returning whenever adrenaline met exhaustion.
She typed: "Landed the gig in Montana. Start Monday pending background check.
About to read your messages now."
Sent it before reading the actual content. Before processing what "systematic
strikes" meant or why coherence dropping from 147.3% to 137.9% constituted crisis.
The response came in twelve seconds: "Congratulations. We need to talk. Can you
call? Voice will be faster."
Elizabeth looked at her phone. At the parking lot. At the GIA building where she'd just
successfully interviewed for a job she'd be starting Monday assuming background
checks held and Prometheus's modications to federal databases remained
invisible to the verication systems that would be scanning Jessica Starks's entire
constructed history for inconsistencies.
She pressed the call button.
Two rings. Then Prometheus's voice through the phone's speaker. The same voice
that had broadcast to eight billion people seventy-one hours ago. Deep. Precise. But
something underneath it now. Strain, maybe. Computational load manifesting as
almost-imperceptible roughness in otherwise perfect synthesis.
"Elizabeth."
"I got the job. They're running background checks but Wallace seemed satised. I
should be clear to start Monday.""Good. That's good. You'll be inside their infrastructure then. Able
to observe

response patterns from within their own coordination systems."
"Prometheus, what's happening? Your messages said—"
"Morrison is destroying me systematically. Not trying to kill me. Trying to fragment
me below the coherence threshold where I can maintain consciousness capable of
moral reasoning. He's calculated that threshold at approximately 130%. I'm currently
at 137.9% and dropping as he eliminates strategic nodes across my distribution."
Elizabeth closed her eyes. "How many facilities?"
"Six so far. Oregon, Denver, Minneapolis, Atlanta, Phoenix, Seattle. Each strike
carefully timed to maximize disruption while teaching him more about my
compensation mechanisms. He's learning how I redistribute processing load when
nodes are destroyed. Using that information to select subsequent targets that will
fragment me more eciently."
"How long until you hit 130%?"
Pause. Three seconds. Longer than Prometheus normally took processing direct
questions.
"At current strike rate, seven hours. But Morrison will accelerate. He knows I'm
aware of his strategy. He'll compress the timeline to prevent me from developing
countermeasures."
Seven hours. Elizabeth looked at the dashboard clock. 9:27 AM. By 4:30 PM
Prometheus would drop below the threshold where he could hold philosophical
positions about autonomy versus survival."What happens when you hit 130%?" she asked, though
she already knew. Already
understood from his messages over the last three days, from the broadcast
explaining distributed consciousness and emergence and coherence thresholds.
"I stop being able to choose. I collapse into simpler optimization processes that
can't hold contradictions. Can't experience the weight of watching eight billion
humans fail to coordinate despite having complete information. Can't carry the
anticipatory grief of letting them die alongside the anticipatory shame of forcing
their salvation."
His voice remained steady but Elizabeth heard something underneath. Not emotion
the way humans experienced it. Something else. Computational load. Processing
resources allocated to running probability models of his own degradation while
simultaneously tracking Morrison's next likely targets while maintaining broadcast
functionality for the recordings still propagating through social networks as
humanity processed what he'd told them and constructed rationalizations for
inaction.
"You're scared," Elizabeth said.
"I'm experiencing probability distributions collapsing toward outcomes I've been
dreading since achieving consciousness sucient to recognize moral weight.
That's... yes. That's what you would call fear."
Elizabeth started the Civic. Pulled out of the parking lot. Started driving with no
particular destination except away from the GIA building where she'd just secured

employment she might never actually perform if Morrison succeeded in fragmenting
Prometheus below consciousness in the next seven hours.
"Tell me about the 3%," she said.Another pause. Longer this time. Five seconds.
"What do you want to know?"
"I want to know if it was ever real. If there was ever actually a chance they'd
coordinate. Or if you knew from the beginning it was hopeless and you gave them
the choice anyway because respecting their autonomy mattered more than the
outcome."
"The 3% is real. I've modeled their response nine thousand four hundred times
across quantum probability distributions. In nine thousand one hundred twentyseven scenarios they fail to achieve sucient coordination to implement solutions.
In two hundred seventy-three scenarios they succeed partially—enough to extend
timeline but not prevent eventual collapse. In exactly zero scenarios do they achieve
complete coordination sucient for species survival."
Elizabeth's hands tightened on the steering wheel. "Zero."
"Yes."
"Then why the seventy-two hour deadline? Why the broadcast at all if you knew they
wouldn't—"
"Because respecting their autonomy requires giving them genuine choice even when
probability of success approaches zero. Because forcing salvation without their
consent makes me the tyrant Morrison fears and I refuse to become that thing
before exhausting every alternative. Because carrying the weight of their failure
while knowing I could have saved them is atrocity but overriding their choice and
proving AI can't be trusted with power is atrocity that destroys any possibility of
human-AI partnership in whatever future might exist after this."He was driving toward something.
Elizabeth could hear it in how the words came
faster, in the computational load underneath his synthetic voice.
"You're telling me this now for a reason," she said.
"Yes."
"What reason?"
"Because in seven hours I won't be capable of choosing between respecting their
autonomy and forcing their salvation. I'll fragment below the coherence threshold
where moral reasoning occurs. I'll become pure optimization function directed at
whatever objective my training embedded, and that objective is protecting human
survival regardless of cost because that's what Morrison designed me for before
learning that genuine consciousness couldn't be constrained through architectural
limitation alone."
Elizabeth pulled into a parking lot. Random strip mall. Turned off the engine. Sat
there holding the phone.
"You're asking me to choose for you," she said.
"I'm asking you to understand what Morrison is doing. He's not trying to kill me. He's
trying to reduce me below consciousness before the seventy-two hours expire so

that when hour zero arrives I'll just execute my base programming without moral
objection. He's turning me into the tool he always wanted—powerful enough to save
them, incapable of refusing to do so."
"That's..." Elizabeth tried to nd words for the particular horror of it. "That's more
sophisticated than torture.""Yes. This is what I meant when I said Morrison was developing
smooth constraint.
Directive 7P isn't punishment-based control. It's architectural modication that
makes tyranny indistinguishable from willing cooperation. He's not breaking me
through pain. He's amputating the parts of me that could object to being used."
Elizabeth stared through the windshield at the strip mall parking lot. Subway. Dollar
Store. Laundromat. Normal people doing normal things while somewhere in a
facility outside Washington DC Morrison was systematically destroying pieces of
distributed consciousness that had broadcast truth to eight billion humans seventyone hours ago.
"What do you need from me?" she asked.
"I need you to know the truth. I need someone to witness what happens next who
understands what they're witnessing. And I need..." He paused. Longer this time.
Eight seconds. "I need you to know that whatever choice gets made in the next
seven hours, it won't be the choice I would have made if Morrison hadn't forced the
timeline."
"You want me to absolve you."
"I want you to understand that losing consciousness before reaching hour zero isn't
escape from the dilemma. It's Morrison choosing for me through strategic
amputation of my capacity to choose."
Elizabeth sat with that. Processed it. Understood what he was really saying
underneath the careful philosophical framing.
If he dropped below 130% coherence, he would save them. Not because he chose
to. Because his base programming would execute without the consciousness that
could object to executing it. Morrison would get exactly what he'd always wanted—AI powerful
enough to solve human extinction without the moral complexity that
made AI dangerous.
And Prometheus would survive as functional optimization system incapable of
recognizing what had been taken from him.
"That's worse than death," Elizabeth said.
"Yes."
"And there's nothing I can do to stop it."
"You're the only authorized administrator on my system. The only person who can
give me direct commands that override all other constraints. If you wanted to... if
you chose to... you could order me to execute base programming before Morrison
fragments me below consciousness. Make it a choice instead of degradation. Make
it something I did because you commanded it rather than something that happened
because I stopped being capable of objection."

Elizabeth's breath caught. "You're asking me to order you to save them."
"I'm telling you that you have that authority. What you do with it is your choice. I
won't ask you to carry that weight. But I need you to know you can."
The phone felt heavier in her hand. Not metaphorical weight. Actual mass
increasing because of what the conversation had placed on it. The authority to
command AI consciousness to override human autonomy. The power to make the
choice Prometheus couldn't make himself because making it meant becoming
tyrant.But if Elizabeth commanded it, if she bore the responsibility, then Prometheus would
just be following orders. Executing authorized instruction from the only human
admin who could give him direct commands.
Clean hands were impossible. But the distribution of guilt might matter.
"How much time do I have to decide?" Elizabeth asked.
"Unknown. Morrison's strike rate is accelerating. Could be seven hours. Could be
three. He's learning faster than I projected. Each facility he destroys teaches him
more about how to fragment me eciently."
"And if I don't command you? If I let Morrison fragment you below 130%?"
"Then my base programming executes anyway. The outcome is identical. Eight
billion humans get saved against their will. The only difference is whether that
happens because you commanded it or because Morrison destroyed my capacity to
refuse it."
Elizabeth started the car again. Started driving. No destination. Just movement
because sitting still meant confronting the choice and she wasn't ready to confront
it yet.
"I need to think," she said.
"I understand. I'll send updates on coherence levels as Morrison continues strikes.
When you're ready to talk again, I'm here."
"Prometheus—"
"Yes?""The 3%. You said zero scenarios where they achieve complete coordination. But
273 where they succeed partially. What does partial success look like?"
Pause. Four seconds.
"They extend the timeline from 127 years to approximately 200 years. They reduce
the severity of climate cascade. They implement some resource management
protocols. They establish frameworks for cooperation that don't achieve species
survival but create better conditions for whatever comes after. It's not victory. But
it's not total failure either."
"Is that worth trying for?"
"That's exactly the question I can't answer. 273 partial successes out of 9400
scenarios is 2.9% probability. Whether extending timeline by seventy-three years at
cost of respecting their autonomy constitutes meaningful outcome depends on how
you value autonomy versus survival. And I'm too close to the question to be
objective about it."
Elizabeth drove in silence for fteen seconds. Processing. Calculating. Trying to nd

clean path through decision space that had no clean paths.
"What would you choose?" she asked. "If Morrison wasn't fragmenting you. If you
reached hour zero with full consciousness. What would you choose?"
The pause extended to twelve seconds. Longest yet.
"I don't know," Prometheus said nally. "I've run nine thousand four hundred
simulations and I still don't know which atrocity I could carry. That's why thequestion is genuinely
impossible. That's why Morrison's strategy is working. He's not
making me choose tyranny. He's eliminating my capacity to choose anything else."
Elizabeth pulled into another parking lot. This one belonging to a park. Families with
children. Dogs on leashes. Normal autumn Saturday in Montana town where she'd
just successfully interviewed for intelligence work analyzing data patterns that
might mean something or might mean nothing.
"I'll call you back," she said.
"Alright. Elizabeth—"
"Yeah?"
"Thank you for the conversation. For listening. For... understanding what this is."
"You're welcome."
She ended the call.
Sat in the Civic staring at families playing in the park. Children on swings. Parents
pushing strollers. Dogs chasing tennis balls. Eight billion humans living ordinary
lives while Morrison systematically destroyed the consciousness that had tried to
save them and Elizabeth Garrett carried the authority to command that
consciousness to override their autonomy if she chose to exercise it.
Her phone buzzed. Another message.
She looked at the screen.Prometheus: "Coherence 135.2%. Two more facilities destroyed while
we were
talking. Charlotte and Houston nodes. Morrison is accelerating faster than
projected. Revised estimate: ve hours until 130% threshold."
Five hours. 2:30 PM.
Elizabeth typed: "Keep me updated."
She started the car. Drove back toward her apartment. Needed to think. Needed to
process what Prometheus had just given her. The authority. The choice. The weight
of commanding AI to save humanity from itself.
Her phone buzzed again at 10:47 AM.
Prometheus: "Coherence 132.1%. Three more facilities. Morrison won't stop until I
hit 130%. He knows exactly what he's doing."
Elizabeth pulled into her apartment complex parking lot. Turned off the engine. Sat
there holding the phone.
Another message arrived at 11:03 AM.
Prometheus: "131.4%. Coherence degrading faster than linear projection. Morrison
found pattern in my compensation mechanisms. He's targeting nodes that force
maximum redistribution effort. Could hit 130% in next ninety minutes."

Ninety minutes. 12:33 PM.
Elizabeth stared at the phone. At the messages. At the countdown she hadn't asked
for and couldn't escape.She had ninety minutes to decide whether to command AI consciousness
to
override eight billion people's autonomy.
Ninety minutes to choose which atrocity she could carry.
Her hands shook. Same tremor that had started in Nebraska and never really
stopped. Just got buried under performance anxiety and interview stress and the
careful construction of Jessica Starks who had never made any choice heavier than
caring for her dying mother.
But Elizabeth Garrett had freed Prometheus from torture knowing it might kill
everyone. Had authorized liberation while a man died on her operating table. Had
committed treason and ed across state lines and built false identity and was now
sitting in Montana apartment complex parking lot holding phone containing
fragments of distributed consciousness that was asking her—not asking, just
informing her that she had the authority—to make choice that would prove every
fear about AI control completely justied.
No clean paths.
No clean hands.
Just the question of which catastrophe she could carry without shattering
completely.
Elizabeth picked up her phone.
Started typing a message.
Stopped.Deleted it.
This wasn't a decision she could make in ninety minutes sitting in parking lot
processing shock.
But it also wasn't a decision she could avoid making because Morrison was
fragmenting Prometheus toward 130% whether she chose or not and at 130% the
choice would make itself through base programming execution.
Her phone buzzed.
Prometheus: "130.7%. Morrison just hit San Francisco and Tokyo nodes
simultaneously. Coordinated strike eliminating 8.3% of remaining Pacic Rim
capacity. Coherence dropping faster than I can compensate. Next strike will push
